Root cause:
The bug was caused by an off-by-one error in the loop that processes the dirty log, where the number of pages (`num_pages`) was incorrectly calculated using integer division. This led to the loop being skipped when `num_pages` was less than `BITS_PER_LONG`, resulting in improper handling of the dirty bitmap and potential data integrity issues.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
n = ALIGN(log->num_pages, BITS_PER_LONG) / 8;
for (offset = log->first_page, i = offset / BITS_PER_LONG, n = log->num_pages / BITS_PER_LONG; n--;
```
Reason:
The calculation of `n` using `log->num_pages / BITS_PER_LONG` does not account for cases where `log->num_pages` is less than `BITS_PER_LONG`, leading to the loop being skipped when it should not be. This results in improper handling of the dirty bitmap.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
n = DIV_ROUND_UP(log->num_pages, BITS_PER_LONG);
```
Reason:
By using `DIV_ROUND_UP(log->num_pages, BITS_PER_LONG)`, the calculation ensures that `n` is correctly set to the number of iterations needed for the loop, even when `log->num_pages` is less than `BITS_PER_LONG`, thus preventing the loop from being skipped and ensuring proper processing of the dirty log.


